Spc><a:spcBef><a:spcPts val="560"/></a:spcBef><a:spcAft><a:spcPts val="0"/></a:spcAft><a:buClr><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:buClr><a:buSzPts val="2800"/><a:buFont typeface="Calibri"/><a:buChar char="•"/></a:pPr><a:r><a:rPr b="0" i="0" lang="en-US" sz="2800" u="none"><a:solidFill><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:solidFill><a:latin typeface="Calibri"/><a:ea typeface="Calibri"/><a:cs typeface="Calibri"/><a:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:rPr><a:t>Still two subunits with mRNA running between</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr/></a:p><a:p><a:pPr algn="l" indent="-342900" lvl="0" marL="342900" marR="0" rtl="0"><a:lnSpc><a:spcPct val="100000"/></a:lnSpc><a:spcBef><a:spcPts val="560"/></a:spcBef><a:spcAft><a:spcPts val="0"/></a:spcAft><a:buClr><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:buClr><a:buSzPts val="2800"/><a:buFont typeface="Calibri"/><a:buChar char="•"/></a:pPr><a:r><a:rPr b="0" i="0" lang="en-US" sz="2800" u="none"><a:solidFill><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:solidFill><a:latin typeface="Calibri"/><a:ea typeface="Calibri"/><a:cs typeface="Calibri"/><a:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:rPr><a:t>In eukaryotes, larger (80S), more complex, contain &gt; 80 proteins</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr/></a:p><a:p><a:pPr algn="l" indent="-342900" lvl="0" marL="342900" marR="0" rtl="0"><a:lnSpc><a:spcPct val="100000"/></a:lnSpc><a:spcBef><a:spcPts val="560"/></a:spcBef><a:spcAft><a:spcPts val="0"/></a:spcAft><a:buClr><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:buClr><a:buSzPts val="2800"/><a:buFont typeface="Calibri"/><a:buChar char="•"/></a:pPr><a:r><a:rPr b="0" i="0" lang="en-US" sz="2800" u="none"><a:solidFill><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:solidFill><a:latin typeface="Calibri"/><a:ea typeface="Calibri"/><a:cs typeface="Calibri"/><a:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:rPr><a:t>Chloroplasts and mitochondria have ribosomes simpler than those in bacteria.</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr/></a:p></p:txBody></p:sp><p:pic><p:nvPicPr><p:cNvPr descr="The composition and mass of ribosomes in bacteria and eukaryotes.  Bacterial ribosome 70S Mr 2.7 x 10[superscript 6] is represented by a yellow large subunit bonded to an orange small subunit as described in image 27-13. An arrow points down to another yellow subunit. On top of this is “50S,” and below this is “Mr1.8 x 10[superscript 6], SSrRNA (12- nucleotides), 235 rRNA (3,200 nucleotides), 36 proteins.” An arrow points down from the small subunit to another small subunit. This is labeled as 30S. Below this is “Mr0.9 x 10 [superscript 6], 16S rRNA (1,540 nucleotides), 21 proteins.” Back up at the top and to the right is the eukaryotic ribosome 80S Mr 4.2 x 10[superscript 6]. This has a pink large subunit and a blue small subunit bonded below. An arrow points down from the large subunit to another large subunit, labeled as 60S. Below this, it states, “Mr2.8 x 10 [superscript 6], 5SrRNA (120 nucleotides), 28SrRNA (4,700 nucleotides), 5.8SrRNA (160 nucleotides), 47 proteins.” An arrow points down from the small subunit to another small subunit labeled as 40S. Below this is “Mr1.4 x 10 (superscript 6), 18SrRNA (1,900 nucleotides), 33 proteins.”" id="305" name="Google Shape;305;p43"/><p:cNvPicPr preferRelativeResize="0"/><p:nvPr/></p:nvPicPr><p:blipFill rotWithShape="1"><a:blip r:embed="rId3"><a:alphaModFix/></a:blip><a:srcRect b="0" l="0" r="0" t="0"/><a:stretch/></p:blipFill><p:spPr><a:xfrm><a:off x="4924425" y="1143000"/><a:ext cx="3609975" cy="5513387"/></a:xfrm><a:prstGeom prst="rect"><a:avLst/></a:prstGeom><a:noFill/><a:ln><a:noFill/></a:ln></p:spPr></p:pic></p:spTree></p:cSld><p:clrMapOvr><a:masterClrMapping/></p:clrMapOvr></p:sld>